 & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =16t+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372666 13194139535887 true] [17592186372665 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535887 true] [17592186372665 1041 "field-1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372665 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372666 13194139535887 true] [17592186372665 1110 #uuid "6e4b3527-f8b3-54b0-a13c-75d3e73ae462" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372666 1034 "16t+12\\cos(t)" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372666 1111 #uuid "e143678e-4dfa-51c9-bd27-7dbfe99013c5" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372668 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1089 "q-315735" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1100 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1113 #uuid "92d1e0da-6675-52b1-9f96-c4f5a35a7942" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372667 1145 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t+\\frac{4}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+\\frac{4}{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =2-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =ma(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372669 13194139535887 true] [17592186372668 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535887 true] [17592186372668 1041 "field-1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372668 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372669 13194139535887 true] [17592186372668 1110 #uuid "350ef28e-1c61-5ea8-aabf-a59f99fbf641" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372669 1034 "2" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372669 1111 #uuid "e1c128e2-f026-54cd-af03-9ccce3af6c0f" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372671 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1089 "q-345206" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1100 "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1113 #uuid "45eb9b77-c6ce-5aa4-801c-a93678ce677f" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372670 1145 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372672 13194139535887 true] [17592186372671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535887 true] [17592186372671 1041 "field-1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372672 13194139535887 true] [17592186372671 1110 #uuid "c72c1987-ec25-54f4-8b2c-27a961d701fa" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372672 1034 "\\frac{2t}{t^2+3}" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372672 1111 #uuid "92aff546-a8cf-506e-8cda-2773ca87f447" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372674 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1089 "q-345207" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1100 "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1113 #uuid "e8a625fd-0a7f-5970-a294-68b09e11cf6f" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372673 1145 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372675 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372676 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1041 "field-1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372676 13194139535887 true] [17592186372674 1110 #uuid "f36f7e29-def4-5e9b-b019-e4536d1d2707" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372675 1034 "8t" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372675 1111 #uuid "6be53d43-1d90-5c86-99f5-e9726f77f703" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372676 1034 "\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372676 1111 #uuid "8dfd070b-2a61-59f4-92b6-41c47413e794" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1017 #edb/ref 17592186372678 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1089 "q-345227" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1100 "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1113 #uuid "1f09fa8f-3853-500b-aee6-60e0f296f95d" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372677 1145 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =2{e}^{\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =ma(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)|=|1|=1\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372679 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186372680 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1041 "field-1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1104 #edb/ref 17592186372680 13194139535887 true] [17592186372678 1110 #uuid "761a5017-d38e-51fc-b9c8-f7283907b94a" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372679 1034 "2" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372679 1111 #uuid "9c9172c6-2360-508f-8ace-832111819474" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372680 1034 "1" 13194139535887 true] [17592186372680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535887 true] [17592186372680 1111 #uuid "c09c9dd6-4afc-5e75-89df-a1fb2517f727" 1319413953